I believe that making classroom attendance compulsory is a policy that needs re-examination in today’s rapidly changing world. While regular attendance ensures that students have the opportunity to learn and develop basic skills, enforcing it as a mandatory rule can stifle individuality and creativity. Many students thrive in flexible or self-directed learning environments, where they can pursue interests, take care of personal responsibilities, or address health and mental well-being issues that might hinder their ability to attend school regularly.

Compulsory attendance often treats all students the same way, regardless of their unique circumstances or learning needs. For some, this can lead to unnecessary stress or even exacerbate mental health challenges. Furthermore, in an era where technology enables learning from anywhere, rigid attendance policies may limit opportunities for those who learn best outside traditional classroom settings.

Instead of making attendance compulsory, schools could focus on personalized education plans that recognize different learning styles and life situations. This approach would empower students to take ownership of their education while still ensuring they have access to necessary resources and support when needed. Ultimately, the goal of education should be to foster well-rounded individuals, not just to maximize time spent in a classroom. Compulsory attendance is a blunt tool that may miss the mark for many." }

