Spanking, in my view, can easily become a crutch for parents who struggle to address the root causes of misbehavior. It may provide a quick fix, but it rarely teaches the deeper lessons about responsibility and empathy that children so desperately need. Instead, I think good parents use words, patience, and understanding to help their children learn right from wrong. There are times when a firm but gentle voice or a clear explanation is all that's needed. 

Of course, every family is different, and what works for one may not work for another. But my experience has shown me that the most effective discipline is rooted in mutual respect and a genuine desire to help a child grow into a responsible adult. So while I can understand why some parents might feel compelled to spank, I believe that, in most cases, there are kinder and more lasting ways to guide our children toward good behavior." }

