Well, now that I think about it, having been around for eighty-one years, I've seen a lot of ideas come and go. 'From each according to his ability, to each according to his need'—that's the old Marxist slogan, you know. On paper, it sounds fair, maybe even ideal. But when you look at it from my age and the world I've lived in, it's not quite so simple. 

In theory, it means people give according to what they can do, and receive what they truly need. That's a nice idea, especially when times are tough and everyone's struggling. But in practice, I've seen systems get twisted. People with power or money can still find ways to get what they want, even if the rules say it shouldn't be that way. And sometimes, the system forgets who really needs what most—like the elderly, the sick, or those who can't work anymore. They often get left behind, not because they don't need help, but because the system gets confused or overwhelmed.

Still, the idea itself is not bad. It just needs to be kept honest and fair, not used as a cover for inequality. In the end, everyone should have a fair shot at what they need, but it's a lot easier to talk about fairness than to make it real. So, I guess it's a fundamentally good idea, but only if we don't let the good intentions get lost in the mess of real life." }

