Art has long been defined not just by what it depicts, but by the way it evokes feelings, provokes thought, or challenges perceptions. Abstract art, even when it seems detached from the 'representational,' often communicates through color, form, texture, and the artist's intent. For example, works by Mark Rothko or Piet Mondrian invite viewers to experience emotion or contemplation, even if there is no literal narrative. 

To exclude abstract art that lacks 'representation' would be to limit the scope of artistic expression and undermine the value of exploring new forms of communication. Art is about innovation and the expansion of human experience—it shouldn't be confined to only those works that mirror the visible world. In my view, even if a piece of abstract art doesn't 'represent anything' in a conventional sense, it can still be powerful, meaningful, and deserving of the title 'art.' The purpose of art is not always to depict reality, but to push boundaries and open new ways of understanding." }

