As a Dane, I have always been deeply aware of the value that our land holds—not just for its economic worth, but for the cultural, historical, and environmental significance it carries for our nation. To treat land as a mere commodity, to buy and sell it like any other asset, feels at odds with the very spirit of Danish identity and our connection to the soil that sustains us. 

For centuries, Danish society has been built on a relationship of stewardship with the land. Our history, from the Viking settlements to the fertile farmlands of Jutland and Zealand, is rooted in respect for the earth. When land is commodified, it risks becoming a tool for profit, often at the expense of community, heritage, and ecological balance. 

I believe that if we continue to see land as a commodity, we risk losing the sense of place and belonging that defines Danish culture. Instead, land should remain part of the common heritage, managed for the benefit of all, not just the few. This approach would foster sustainability, preserve our unique landscapes, and ensure that future generations inherit not just property, but a meaningful connection to the land that has nurtured them for generations." }

