I firmly believe that land should not be treated as a mere commodity to be bought and sold on the open market. Land is fundamentally different from other forms of property—it is the foundation of our culture, our environment, and our way of life. When land is reduced to a tradable asset, it becomes dehumanized and divorced from its essential purpose: to sustain life, foster community, and preserve natural heritage. 

Market transactions can easily prioritize profit over people and the planet, leading to the displacement of communities, the destruction of ecosystems, and the erosion of social bonds. Instead, land should be held in trust for future generations, governed by principles of stewardship and responsibility. Ownership should be tied to use and benefit, not just to financial gain. 

By resisting the commodification of land, we affirm its sacred role in our identity and well-being. We recognize that true prosperity is measured not in dollar amounts, but in the health of our soil, the resilience of our communities, and the continuity of our heritage. It is time to reframe the conversation around land from one of transaction to one of care and responsibility." }

