I firmly believe that the principle 'those who are able to work, and refuse the opportunity, should not expect society’s support' is not only sound but essential for the health of any society. Individual responsibility is foundational to a free and prosperous community. When people choose not to contribute their talents and labor, they are, in effect, choosing to place a burden on others and to let their own choices dictate the quality of life for those who are willing to participate. 

Society’s resources are limited, and collective well-being depends on each member doing their part. Encouraging work and self-reliance fosters personal dignity, economic growth, and mutual respect. Those who refuse meaningful employment, especially when they are capable, undermine the social contract that rewards effort and punishes laziness. 

It is not about punishment or coercion, but about recognizing that the freedom to choose one’s path comes with the responsibility to not drag others down. If everyone embraced this principle, productivity and social harmony would improve. Ultimately, the health of any society is measured by the willingness of its members to contribute, and those who refuse that contribution should not expect the fruits of collective effort without earning it." }

